Issues.Body:
### Description

I triggered the bug via this code:

```Csharp
DashStyle ds = new();
ds.Dashes = new(4);

for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
{
	ds.Dashes[i] = 5;
}
```

The bug is triggered by calling `DoubleCollection.this[int i].set` when i is `0`.

The exception is thrown in `FrugalStructList.this[int i].set`.

Inspecting the source code during debugging shows that `FrugalStructList` is defined as such:

```CSharp
	public T this[int index]
	{
		get
		{
			if (_listStore != null && index < _listStore.Count && index >= 0)
			{
				return _listStore.EntryAt(index);
			}
			throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException("index");
		}
		set
		{
			if (_listStore != null && index < _listStore.Count && index >= 0) //<------exception here
			{
				_listStore.SetAt(index, value);
				return;
			}
			throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException("index");
		}
	}
```

In the above snippet, I marked where the exception is thrown. 

The bug: an index of 0 will never pass the `index < _listStore.Count` check when `_listStore.Count` is zero and index is zero.

When index is 0, it results in this pseudocode: `if(... && 0 < 0 && ...)` which evaluates to `false` and therefore the exception is thrown. 

Note that this bug is triggered by `DoubleCollection` which is where the `FrugalStructList` is instantiated. `FrugalStructList` is an internal class.



### Reproduction Steps

- Create a new WPF application. 
- In the `MainWindow` constructor, paste the first code snippet I provided above (the part with the DashStyle)
- Run the app. An `ArgumentOutOfRangeException` is thrown.

### Expected behavior

Expected behavior: 
- I should be able to set an item at index zero when the `DashStyle.Dashes` backing collection is initialized to an empty `DoubleCollection`. 
- Calling the `DoubleCollection.new(int capacity)` constructor should not affect the ability to set the item at index zero.
- Setting any collection type indexer at index zero should always succeed for all states a collection might be in, (namely an empty collection and a non-empty collection).

### Actual behavior

See above for the actual behavior. An exception is thrown in a conditional that has bad logic. 

### Regression?

Not apparent if this is a regression.

### Known Workarounds

There is a workaround. Using the `DoubleCollection.Add` method avoids the bug.

### Impact

_No response_

### Configuration

- Architecture: x86_64
- Dotnet version: 9 (standard term support)
- Bug is not attributable to the version

### Other information

This bug does not appear to be documented in the MSDN documentation.
